The following list of buildings represent a special class of potential fire hazard and must have the building plans reviewed and approved by the county fire department prior to issuance of a city building permit. This review will be for the purpose of assuring the provision of adequate fire safety features in the design, construction and operation of the proposed building or addition. This section applies to proposed new buildings to be constructed in the city, as well as remodeling projects requiring a permit; provided, that the review of remodeling projects shall be limited to that portion of the structure for which the permit is being obtained. Buildings requiring plan approval shall include:
(1) All commercial buildings;
(2) Buildings or structures more than three stories in height;
(3) Buildings which have a capacity of 75 or more persons on floors other than the first or ground floor;
(4) Buildings having a capacity of more than 100 persons;
(5) Buildings having a gross area of 22,500 square feet on any one floor;
(6) Buildings over two stories in height and used for residence by three or more families;
(7) Buildings used or intended for use as hotels, apartments, dormitories, convents, monasteries, churches, schools, colleges, universities, academies, hospitals, sanitariums, insane asylums, orphanages, theatres, public assembly halls, lecture halls, auditoriums, dancehalls, recreation halls, armories, racetracks, stadiums and grandstands, amusement park structures, nursing, convalescent and old age homes, motels, motor hotels, motor courts, mercantile establishments; and
(8) All other buildings adjacent to any such building specified in this section, whether occupied or unoccupied.
(Code 1986, § 4-2-3)

